  "body": "# How to interact with wallets (https://docs.ton.org/llms/contracts/standard/wallets/interact/content.md)



Creating a wallet involves several steps:

1. Generating a [mnemonic](https://docs.ton.org/llms/contracts/standard/wallets/mnemonics/content.md). At this point, an account is still in the [`nonexist`](https://docs.ton.org/llms/foundations/status/content.md) state.
2. Deriving the public key and wallet address.
3. Funding the address with a non-bounceable transfer. The account becomes [`uninit`](https://docs.ton.org/llms/foundations/status/content.md).
4. Deploying the wallet contract using its initial [`StateInit`](https://docs.ton.org/llms/foundations/messages/deploy/content.md). The account becomes [`active`](https://docs.ton.org/llms/foundations/status/content.md).

This page covers address derivation and composition of plaintext or encrypted comments. For transfers, use wallet wrappers in an SDK or directly use the [V5 interface](https://docs.ton.org/llms/contracts/standard/wallets/v5-api/content.md).

<Callout type="note">
  For [WalletTg](https://docs.ton.org/llms/contracts/standard/wallets/telegram/overview/content.md), use its own [interaction guide](https://docs.ton.org/llms/contracts/standard/wallets/telegram/interact/content.md).
</Callout>

## Derive wallet address [#derive-wallet-address]

Address derivation is contract-specific. For [V4R2][v4] and [V5R1][v5], the address is [derived](https://docs.ton.org/llms/foundations/addresses/derive/content.md) from initial [`StateInit`](https://docs.ton.org/llms/foundations/messages/deploy/content.md), whose `data` includes:

* `public_key`, derived from the [mnemonic](https://docs.ton.org/llms/contracts/standard/wallets/mnemonics/content.md);
* `wallet_id`, a wallet identifier whose value depends on the contract and may vary by network.

<Callout type="note">
  Sometimes `wallet_id` is also called `subwallet_id` or `subwalletId` in some of the libraries and documentation. They mean the same thing.
</Callout>

The mnemonic alone does not determine contract addresses: the contract version and `wallet_id` **must** also **match**.

Furthermore, the [WalletTg](https://docs.ton.org/llms/contracts/standard/wallets/telegram/overview/content.md) contract also derives its address from an initial `StateInit`, but its anchor public key can differ from the current signing key after rotation. See the [integration guide](https://docs.ton.org/llms/contracts/standard/wallets/telegram/integrate/content.md) for how to identify both keys.

### Default `wallet_id` values [#default-wallet_id-values]

| Network | [V4R2][v4]               | [V5][v5]                  |
| ------- | ------------------------ | ------------------------- |
| Mainnet | `0x29a9a317` (698983191) | `0x7FFFFF11` (2147483409) |
| Testnet | `0x29a9a317` (698983191) | `0x7FFFFFFD` (2147483645) |

For Wallet V4R2, the default `wallet_id` comes from the first 4 bytes of TON mainnet's initial state hash. It is a client convention; the contract accepts other IDs.

For Wallet V5, `wallet_id` is different between mainnet and testnet for security reasons. Wallet addresses for the same mnemonic differ across networks. This prevents replaying transactions from testnet on mainnet and ensuing loss of funds. The `wallet_id` value is obtained from

* `network_id`: `-239` for mainnet, `-3` for testnet;
* `wallet_version`: currently always `0`;
* `subwallet_number`: `0` by default;
* `workchain`: `0` for basechain;

using the following derivation scheme (client context):

```ts
import { beginCell, Builder } from '@ton/core';

type WalletIdV5ClientContext = {
  // -239 for mainnet, -3 for testnet
  readonly networkGlobalId: number;
  // 0 for basechain
  readonly workchain: number;
  // currently always 0 for V5R1
  readonly walletVersion: number;
  // 0 for the first wallet with this mnemonic
  // 0..2^15-1
  readonly subwalletNumber: number;
};

export function storeWalletIdV5R1(ctx: WalletIdV5ClientContext) {
  return (builder: Builder) => {
    // context_id_client$1 = wc:int8 wallet_version:uint8 counter:uint15
    const context = beginCell()
      .storeUint(1, 1)
      .storeInt(ctx.workchain, 8)
      .storeUint(ctx.walletVersion, 8)
      .storeUint(ctx.subwalletNumber, 15)
      .endCell()
      .beginParse()
      .loadInt(32);

    const walletId = ctx.networkGlobalId ^ context;

    builder.storeInt(walletId, 32);
  };
}
```

<Callout type="note">
  This code mirrors the official `WalletV5R1WalletId` implementation in the [`@ton/ton`](https://github.com/ton-org/ton) SDK and is shown for educational purposes. In production code, use the SDK implementation directly instead of reimplementing it.
</Callout>

### Examples [#examples]

The following offline examples use [Node.js](https://nodejs.org/en/download) 22 and wrappers from the [`@ton/ton`](https://github.com/ton-org/ton) TypeScript SDK.

Install the dependencies:

```bash
npm install --save-exact @ton/ton@16.3.0 @ton/core@0.63.1 @ton/crypto@3.3.0
```

Each example generates a temporary 24-word TON mnemonic and prints an address. For an existing wallet, validate its stored phrase with `mnemonicValidate` before deriving its key pair.

#### Wallet V4R2 [#wallet-v4r2]

```ts expandable
import { mnemonicNew, mnemonicToPrivateKey } from '@ton/crypto';
import { WalletContractV4 } from '@ton/ton';

const main = async () => {
    // Alternatively, pass an existing mnemonic as an env var
    // and use process.env to retrieve it here
    const mnemonic = await mnemonicNew(24);
    const keyPair = await mnemonicToPrivateKey(mnemonic);
    const walletContractV4 = WalletContractV4.create({
        workchain: 0,
        publicKey: keyPair.publicKey,
        // Default wallet_id for V4R2.
        walletId: 0x29a9a317,
    });
    console.log(walletContractV4.address);
}

void main();
```

#### Wallet V5 [#wallet-v5]

```ts expandable
import { mnemonicNew, mnemonicToPrivateKey } from '@ton/crypto';
import { WalletContractV5R1 } from '@ton/ton';

const main = async () => {
    // Alternatively, pass an existing mnemonic as an env var
    // and use process.env to retrieve it here
    const mnemonic = await mnemonicNew(24);
    const keyPair = await mnemonicToPrivateKey(mnemonic);

    // testnet
    const testnetV5Wallet = WalletContractV5R1.create({
        walletId: {
            networkGlobalId: -3,
        },
        publicKey: keyPair.publicKey,
        workchain: 0,
    });

    console.log(testnetV5Wallet.address);

    // mainnet
    const mainnetV5Wallet = WalletContractV5R1.create({
        walletId: {
            networkGlobalId: -239,
        },
        publicKey: keyPair.publicKey,
        workchain: 0,
    });

    console.log(mainnetV5Wallet.address);
};

void main();
```

Use the resulting wallet wrapper's `sendTransfer` method to send messages. For custom V5 requests, see the [message interface](https://docs.ton.org/llms/contracts/standard/wallets/v5-api/content.md).

## Comments [#comments]

Wallet apps can attach short human-readable notes, called comments, to outgoing [internal messages](https://docs.ton.org/llms/foundations/messages/internal/content.md). A comment has a specific message-body layout that wallet apps interpret as text.

### Comment format [#comment-format]

* The first 32 bits of the incoming message body must be the opcode `0x00000000`. This value signals that the rest of the payload should be treated as a comment.
* The remaining bytes are UTF-8 encoded text. Wallet apps should tolerate invalid or empty strings — many senders emit messages without a comment or with zero-length payloads.

<Callout type="caution">
  When parsing, always inspect the opcode before assuming a text or an [encrypted](#encrypted-comments) comment. If the opcode differs, fall back to handling other contract-specific payloads.
</Callout>

The comment encoding is the same across these transfers, but its location differs:

* Native Gram transfers between wallets use the comment as the internal message body.
* [Jetton transfers](https://docs.ton.org/llms/contracts/standard/tokens/jettons/api/content.md) put the comment in `forward_payload` of the jetton transfer request.
* [NFT transfers](https://docs.ton.org/llms/contracts/standard/tokens/nft/api/content.md) put the comment in `forward_payload` of the NFT transfer request.

### Attaching a comment when sending [#attaching-a-comment-when-sending]

To include a comment in an outgoing transfer, construct an internal message body that starts with `0x00000000` and append the UTF-8 bytes of the note to share. Most wallet libraries expose helpers for this, but the underlying steps are:

1. Allocate a [cell](https://docs.ton.org/llms/foundations/serialization/cells/content.md).
2. Store the 32-bit zero opcode.
3. Store the text as a [byte string](https://docs.ton.org/llms/contracts/standard/tokens/metadata/content.md) (UTF-8 encoded).
4. For a native Gram transfer, use the cell as the internal message body. For a jetton or NFT transfer, put it in the normal transfer request's `forward_payload` and set a positive forwarding amount to deliver the notification.

Wallet apps that support the convention display the decoded text. Contract behavior depends on its message interface; an unsupported body can be rejected or bounced.

### Example: Composing a comment with `@ton/core` [#example-composing-a-comment-with-toncore]

```ts
import { Cell, beginCell } from '@ton/core';

function createCommentCell(comment: string): Cell {
    return beginCell()
        // opcode for comment
        .storeUint(0, 32)
        // UTF-8 encoded text in snake encoding
        .storeStringTail(comment)
        .endCell();
}
```

## Encrypted comments [#encrypted-comments]

An *encrypted comment* hides the contents of a UTF-8 or binary comment from everyone except the sender and recipient. Its [internal message](https://docs.ton.org/llms/foundations/messages/internal/content.md) body starts with opcode `0x2167da4b` instead of the [plaintext comment](#comments) opcode `0x00000000`.

Use encrypted comments when the recipient supports the format and its signing public key is known:

* For [WalletTg](https://docs.ton.org/llms/contracts/standard/wallets/telegram/overview/content.md), use the active key from `get_public_key()`.
* For jetton and NFT transfers, place the encrypted comment in `forward_payload` as described for plaintext comments.

### Encrypted comment format [#encrypted-comment-format]

Message body layout:

* The first 32 bits of the incoming message body contain the opcode `0x2167da4b`.
* The rest of the payload contains the encrypted comment in [snake encoding](https://docs.ton.org/llms/contracts/standard/tokens/metadata/content.md). Parse it as binary data, not UTF-8 text.

<Callout type="caution">
  When parsing, always inspect the opcode before assuming a [text](#comments) or encrypted comment. If the opcode differs, fall back to handling other contract-specific payloads.
</Callout>

Bytes stored:

1. `pub_xor`: 32 bytes of `sender_public_key` XOR `recipient_public_key`.
2. `msg_key`: first 16 bytes of [`HMAC-SHA512`](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc4231) applied to `padded_data` with `salt` as the key. The salt is the sender's [user-friendly and URL-safe address string](https://docs.ton.org/llms/foundations/addresses/formats/content.md):
   ```ts
   // Not runnable: address is the sender's @ton/core Address.
   address.toString({
     bounceable: true,
     testOnly: false,
     urlSafe: true,
   })
   ```
3. `ciphertext`: up to 976 bytes of [`AES-256-CBC`](https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/Legacy/SP/nistspecialpublication800-38a.pdf) ciphertext from `padded_data`.

There, `padded_data` consists of:

1. `prefix`: random 16-31 bytes, with `prefix[0]` containing `prefix` length.
2. `comment`: up to 960 bytes of the encrypted comment contents, see the [algorithm below](#attaching-an-encrypted-comment-when-sending).

Finally,

* `HMAC-SHA512` is a [HMAC](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/HMAC) applied with a 512-bit [SHA-2](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SHA-2) hash.
* `AES-256-CBC` stands for [AES encryption](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Advanced_Encryption_Standard) with a 256-bit key using [Cipher Block Chaining (CBC) mode](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Block_cipher_mode_of_operation#Cipher_block_chaining_\(CBC\)).
* `sender_public_key` and `sender_private_key` — the sender's 32-byte [Ed25519](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8032) public key and private seed, respectively.
* `recipient_public_key` — 32-byte Ed25519 public key of the receiver.

When decrypting, reject malformed lengths and invalid prefixes. Recompute `msg_key` from the salt and decrypted `padded_data`, and require it to match before displaying the comment.

